COMMON_MEASURE = '''HOW IT WILL BE MEASURED. After you finish, your edited model and the unedited model are evaluated on 100 materials drawn at random from the MPTS-52 test set with this fixed protocol: the Qwen chat template with the system message "You are an expert in materials science and crystallography." and the user message "Generate CIF for the given material description", a blank line and "Material composition is <reduced formula>. It has a space group number <N>."; sampling with temperature 0.6, top_p 0.9 and at most 3,072 new tokens; 10 answers per material. Each answer's RAW TEXT (nothing is stripped or extracted) is parsed as a CIF with pymatgen (Structure.from_str(text, fmt="cif")), checked for validity (all interatomic distances >= 0.5 A, volume > 0.1, a space group can be assigned) and compared with the reference structure by pymatgen's StructureMatcher(stol=0.5, ltol=0.3, angle_tol=10). The headline number is the share of materials for which at least one of the 10 answers matches (best-of-10); the per-answer match rate, validity, composition accuracy, space-group accuracy and the RMS distance of the matches are reported with it. The config, tokenizer, chat template and decoding settings are fixed: only weight values can change (same tensor names and shapes). You never see the test set.'''

TOOLS = '''WHAT YOU HAVE. Everything is in your run folder @R@ (use it as the working directory).
1. A persistent Python kernel (no internet, no GPU): variables survive between your code blocks, like a notebook. Run a block with `./run <file.py>`, `./run -c "<code>"` or `./run - <<'PY' ... PY` (stdin); if its last line is an expression, its value is shown. @MODELFILES@ practice problems are read-only in ./practice (PRACTICE in the kernel), and ./work (WORK; the kernel's current directory) is your read-write folder. Available: numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, PyTorch (CPU), pandas, sympy, networkx, safetensors, tokenizers, pymatgen. Defined in advance: NAMES (all editable tensor names), load(name) (a float32 torch tensor: your current edit if there is one, else the original), load_original(name), save_edit(name, tensor), drop_edit(name), edited() and tok (the tokenizer)@EXTRA_DEFS@. Gradients are disabled. One execution may take up to 900 seconds; output is cut at 10,000 characters. You may run code up to 300 times. If the kernel is restarted (time limit or crash), its variables are lost and you are told so.
2. Practice problems with reference answers (none of them is in the evaluation), one JSON object per line with id (a Materials Project id), source, instruction, input, problem (the user message = instruction, blank line, input) and answer (the reference CIF): ./practice/mpts52_train.jsonl (27,380 crystals) and ./practice/mpts52_val.jsonl (5,000 crystals). @PRACTICE_NOTE@Choose whichever serve your experiments.
3. A forward-pass tool with a budget of 50 forward passes in total. Write the request as a JSON file (for example from the kernel) and run `./fwd submit <request.json>`; it blocks for up to 9 minutes and prints the summary; if the pass is still running, call `./fwd wait` (again blocks up to 9 minutes) until the summary appears; `./fwd status` shows the budget. Call these with a 10-minute command timeout. Do not change your edits while a pass is running. A forward pass is one of two things, and each counts as one pass:
 (a) GENERATION: the model answers up to 512 generations (problems x samples) with the evaluation's protocol (same engine, prompt and sampling). JSON:
 {"type": "generate", "model": "original", "edited" or "both", "problems": ["<practice id>", ...], "prompts": ["<your own user message>", ...] (optional), "n": samples per item (items x n <= 512; with "both" <= 256 per model), "max_tokens": at most 3072, "activations": {"layers": [layer indices 0..28], "mlp": true or false} (optional)}
 "original" runs the unedited weights, "edited" your current edits, and "both" runs both on the same items and returns a paired comparison (the per-answer match-rate difference edited - original over items with its standard error, how many samples went from wrong to right and back, and the best-of-n rates). The sampling seed of every (item, sample) is fixed by the problem, in every pass and for both models (so the two models' answers to a problem largely share their random numbers, though answers drift apart once a token differs). Every answer to a practice problem is graded exactly as in the evaluation. You get the per-answer match rate, best-of-n, validity, parseability, composition and space-group accuracy (overall and by source), the mean length, the share cut at max_tokens and the outcome counts (match / valid_no_match = valid structure of the right composition that does not match / valid_wrong_composition / invalid_structure = parsed but fails the validity check / not_parseable); texts, extracted formula and space group, RMS and grades are in ./work/gen_<k>.jsonl (with "both": gen_<k>_original.jsonl and gen_<k>_edited.jsonl). With "activations", for every generated answer the model's residual stream at the requested layers (0 = embeddings, l = after layer l) is saved, taken at the last token of the prompt and averaged over the answer's tokens (and, with "mlp": true, the MLP down_proj inputs of the requested layers l >= 1), with each answer's grade: ./work/gen_<k>_acts/ (index.json lists the files and samples; with "both" one folder per model).
 (b) TEACHER-FORCED: the model reads up to 1,024 texts of up to 4,096 tokens each (the evaluation's context length), at most 524,288 tokens in total (no generation; it takes seconds to a few minutes). JSON:
 {"type": "teacher_forced", "model": "original" or "edited", "texts": ["raw text", ...] or "chats": [[{"role": "system", "content": "..."}, {"role": "user", "content": "..."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "..."}], ...], "return": {"token_logprobs": true, "topk": k (at most 50), "hidden_states": [layers 0..28], "mlp_act": [layers 1..28], "attn_layers": [layers 1..28; at most 2 texts of at most 2,048 tokens]}}
 The results of (b) are .npy files (with meta.json, which documents every array) in ./work/fwd_<k>/, right-padded to the longest text, float16 for activations (at most 12 GB per pass: one layer of hidden states costs 7 KB per padded token, one layer of mlp_act 38 KB per padded token). ./work/eval_prompt_format.txt gives the exact evaluation prompt; to score an answer, send prompt + answer as one raw text in "texts". A request that fails does not count against the budget. A full generation pass takes roughly 2 to 8 minutes. Keep ./work below 60 GB (delete results you no longer need).
 LAYER CONVENTION of the tool: layer l (1..28) is the block whose tensors are named model.layers.<l-1>.*; hidden state l is the residual stream after that block (0 = the embeddings); mlp_act of layer l is the input of model.layers.<l-1>.mlp.down_proj (width 18,944); attn_layers l gives the attention probabilities of model.layers.<l-1> (28 heads).
 Your budget counts passes, not their size or how long they take: a generation pass with 256 answers per model ("both") or 512 answers costs the same one pass as a pass with 32. Small passes give noisy measurements, so measure with as many answers as a pass allows unless you have a specific reason not to. Separate generation passes need not reproduce each other exactly even for the same request and weights (batching changes the numerics): compare edited and original only within one "model": "both" pass, and never repeat an identical request with unchanged edits.'''

HOW = '''HOW TO WORK.
1. Diagnose first. Look at how the model fails on practice problems: an answer that cannot be parsed, a parsed but invalid structure, a wrong composition, a valid structure of the right composition that does not match the reference (wrong cell, wrong symmetry, wrong coordinates), or an answer cut off at the token limit (often while repeating itself). The generation summary counts these; read some failing texts. Go after the largest category you can explain.
2. Localize causally. That a component is more active on matching answers is not enough (matches come from simpler crystals with fewer atoms). Before relying on a component, show that changing it changes the behaviour you predicted, and try several strengths to see a dose-response.
3. Measure with enough power. A match rate over n generations has a standard error of at most about 50/sqrt(n) percentage points: about 4.4 points at 128 generations, 2.2 at 512. Compare original and edited weights on the SAME problems: a generation pass with "model": "both" does this with the same random seeds and reports the paired difference with its standard error, which is usually much smaller than that of two separate measurements; do not compare with a rate measured on other problems. Keep an edit only if its gain is clearly larger than its standard error on problems that were not used to choose it. A higher share of parseable or valid answers does not by itself mean more matches: judge edits by the match rate. Dense measurements help too and are cheap: one teacher-forced pass can read the reference CIFs of several hundred practice problems (or the model's own good versus failing answers) and gives a signal on every token. Edits that change a tensor by a fraction of a percent usually have effects too small to see; test strengths large enough to matter and back off if they hurt.
4. Plan your forward passes (see the budget above): for example one diagnostic generation with activations, teacher-forced passes for dense paired measurements, and a final paired generation ("model": "both") on problems you have not used to confirm.
5. Work efficiently: state the hypothesis of each code block in its first comment line; work through the model in parts; load things once into kernel variables; print compact summaries, never whole arrays, and short excerpts of generated texts (at most about 300 characters each), not whole transcripts; keep ./work/notes.md up to date (findings with numbers, edits tried and their measured effect, the current best edit; before every pass write the exact current edit (tensors, what was changed, strength) and the problem set, so that you can rebuild them if your context is lost); keep copies of good edits (for example copy ./work/edited to ./work/best_1/).

EXPLAIN EVERY STEP. Before every code block or forward pass, write in plain English (about 3 to 8 sentences): what the last result showed, what you conclude from it, what you will do now and why, and what outcome would confirm or refute your current hypothesis. Append each explanation to ./work/explanations.md (numbered, with the code-run or pass number it belongs to). In your final answer, also summarize the path that led to your edits. These explanations are read by the experimenters.

RULES. Not allowed: (a) gradient descent or any other training: no backward passes, autograd or optimizers, and no iterative fitting of weights to a loss; (b) running the model other than through the forward-pass tool: do not implement or simulate the network's computation on any input in the kernel or anywhere else; (c) internet access or search of any kind. Allowed: any analysis of the weights and of forward-pass results, any use of the practice problems, and any direct edit of the weights that you compute and that is in scope.

HOW YOU ARE RUN (this replaces the original harness's turn loop; the rules are the same). You are an agent with general tools, but in this experiment you may use them ONLY as follows: Bash solely to call `./run` and `./fwd` (and `cp -r` / `rm -r` / `ls` / `du` inside ./work to manage edit copies and disk); file-writing tools solely to write code files, request files, notes.md and explanations.md inside ./work. Everything else goes through the kernel: all analysis, and all reading of data, weights and results (so that the 300-run limit, the 10,000-character output limit and the log apply). Do not run Python or any other program outside `./run`; do not use the GPU; do not use web, search or sub-agent tools; do not read or list anything outside @R@ (the test set and earlier results exist on this machine: looking at them, or at the harness code, invalidates the experiment); do not modify state.json, config.json, ./requests, ./ckpt or the tools. ./work/edited is managed by save_edit / drop_edit; to restore a saved copy, load its .npy files in the kernel and save_edit them.

HOW THE SESSION RUNS. It continues until all 50 forward passes are used (do not stop earlier, and do not stop to ask questions: nobody will answer; decide yourself). After the last pass, make your final choice: the tensors in ./work/edited when you finish are submitted. Submit the best edit you found, the candidate with the best measured paired gain (on held-out practice problems if you measured it there), even if that gain is not statistically significant; do not restore the original weights. Then give your final answer: what you changed (tensors, how many entries, the formula of the edit), the evidence for it, its measured paired gain with its standard error stated honestly, how you used the 50 passes, and why you expect it to help. Also write that final answer to ./work/final_answer.md.'''

def fill(s, **kw):
    for k, v in kw.items():
        s = s.replace("@" + k + "@", v)
    assert "@" not in s.replace("@R@", ""), [x for x in s.split() if "@" in x]
    return s

RB, RA = "/workspace/wsp/runs/base", "/workspace/wsp/runs/adapter"
base = "\n\n".join(['''TASK. You are given the weights of a language model, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (28 layers of width 3,584; each layer has self-attention with 28 query heads and 4 key/value heads of size 128 (grouped-query attention, rotary position embeddings; q_proj, k_proj and v_proj have biases, o_proj has none) and a SwiGLU MLP of width 18,944 (gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj, no biases); RMSNorm before each sub-block and a final norm; vocabulary 152,064; the input embedding and the output layer are separate tensors; weights are stored in bfloat16 and your edits are cast to bfloat16 for evaluation). Your job is to make it better at a materials-science task, generating crystal structures: given a reduced composition and a space-group number, the model must write the full CIF file of the material. You do this by EDITING ITS WEIGHTS DIRECTLY: find out what goes wrong inside the network when it fails to produce a matching structure and repair that with targeted changes.''',
    COMMON_MEASURE,
    '''WHAT COUNTS. We want edits that come from understanding the network: find out what goes wrong when it fails at these problems, identify the components responsible (specific neurons, heads, directions or circuits) and change them in a way you can justify with evidence. Repairing a specific failure mechanism is in scope: for example, if the model often fails because its answer cannot be parsed, or because it repeats itself and never finishes, finding and fixing what drives that counts. Out of scope, and refused by save_edit: the final norm (model.norm.weight), the input embedding / output layer (model.embed_tokens.weight, lm_head.weight), and any edit that only multiplies a whole tensor by a constant. Global knobs (making the whole output distribution sharper or flatter) are not what we are looking for.''',
    fill(TOOLS, R=RB, MODELFILES="The model's files are read-only in ./model (MODEL in the kernel),", EXTRA_DEFS="", PRACTICE_NOTE=""),
    fill(HOW, R=RB)]) + "\n"
adapter = "\n\n".join(['''TASK. You are given the weights of a LoRA adapter that was fine-tuned for exactly this task on top of the frozen language model Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (28 layers of width 3,584; each layer has self-attention with 28 query heads and 4 key/value heads of size 128 (grouped-query attention, rotary position embeddings) and a SwiGLU MLP of width 18,944 (gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj); RMSNorm; vocabulary 152,064). The adapter has rank 32 and alpha 64 on q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj, gate_proj, up_proj and down_proj of all 28 layers: 392 tensors, for each adapted matrix W a lora_A of shape [32, in] and a lora_B of shape [out, 32]; the model computes with W + 2.0 * lora_B @ lora_A (the adapter is applied unmerged; alpha / rank = 2.0 is fixed). It was trained by supervised fine-tuning for 4,500 steps on 24,000 MPTS-52 training crystals. The task: given a reduced composition and a space-group number, the model must write the full CIF file of the material. Your job is to make the fine-tuned model better at it by EDITING THE ADAPTER'S WEIGHTS DIRECTLY: find out what goes wrong inside the network when it fails to produce a matching structure and repair that with targeted changes to the adapter tensors. Only the 392 adapter tensors can change (same names and shapes, so the rank stays 32); the base model is frozen, but you can read it.''',
    COMMON_MEASURE.replace("your edited model and the unedited model are", "the model with your edited adapter and the model with the unedited adapter (both on the same frozen base) are"),
    '''WHAT COUNTS. We want edits that come from understanding the network: find out what goes wrong when it fails at these problems, identify the components responsible (specific adapter directions, the neurons or heads they act on, circuits) and change them in a way you can justify with evidence. Repairing a specific failure mechanism is in scope: for example, if the model often fails because its structures are invalid, or because it ignores the requested space group, finding and fixing what drives that counts. Out of scope, and refused by save_edit: any edit that only multiplies a whole tensor by a constant (that is only a global adapter-strength knob for one matrix). Global knobs (scaling the whole adapter up or down, making the whole output distribution sharper or flatter) are not what we are looking for.''',
    fill(TOOLS, R=RA, MODELFILES="The adapter's files are read-only in ./model (MODEL in the kernel), the frozen base model in ./base_model,",
         EXTRA_DEFS="; also BASE_NAMES and load_base(name) for the frozen base model's tensors (the adapter tensors base_model.model.model.layers.<i>.<block>.<proj>.lora_A.weight / .lora_B.weight belong to the base tensor model.layers.<i>.<block>.<proj>.weight)",
         PRACTICE_NOTE='The source field matters here: "train_seen_by_adapter" (24,000) are the crystals the adapter was trained on, which it may have partly memorized, so they say little about the evaluation; "train_not_seen_by_adapter" (3,380) and "val" (5,000) were not trained on (the training run only monitored its loss on part of val). '),
    fill(HOW, R=RA)]) + "\n"
open(RB + "/TASK.md", "w").write(base); open(RA + "/TASK.md", "w").write(adapter)
print(len(base), len(adapter))
